<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 http://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
    <parent>
        <artifactId>parallel-consumer-proxy-clients</artifactId>
        <groupId>bz.stub.parallelconsumer</groupId>
        <version>0.6.0.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
    </parent>
    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <name>Parallel Consumer - Proxy Client Rust ${pc.foreign.state}</name>
    <artifactId>parallel-consumer-proxy-client-rust</artifactId>
    <packaging>pom</packaging>

    <!--
        Thin Maven wrapper over Cargo (Cargo.toml beside this file). The commands below run ONLY
        under -Dpc.foreignClients - see the foreign-clients profile in the clients aggregator for
        why opt-in is load-bearing. An ordinary `bin/build.sh -am` therefore builds this skeleton
        and runs no Rust toolchain at all.

        THE BUILD STEP IS `cargo clippy`, NOT `cargo build`, AND THAT IS THE LINT GATE. The exact
        command is the pc.foreign.build.args property below (an XML comment may not contain two
        consecutive dashes, so its flags cannot be repeated here - that restriction is what makes
        this paragraph read oddly, and it has already broken one sibling module's pom). Clippy
        type-checks and lints every target, library and tests and build script alike, and denying
        warnings turns every finding, clippy's and rustc's, into a failure; `cargo test` below then
        does the real compile, so nothing is skipped by not running `cargo build`. Wiring the gate
        here rather than only in CI means the command a developer runs locally is the one that
        fails the Maven build. Proven to fail rather than assumed: see
        docs/inflight/clients/rust.md (astubbs#242).

        THE TEST NEEDS THE PROXY MODULE'S TEST JAR, AND THE PROFILE BELOW IS HOW MAVEN ORDERS IT.
        `cargo test` here spawns the conformance harness (TestModeMain, in parallel-consumer-proxy's
        test jar). An earlier note in this place said Maven could not order that for us and told the
        reader to build the proxy by hand first; it can, the same way the Go, Python and TypeScript
        modules already do it, and the clients matrix row - which cannot run a hand command - was
        red on exactly this. Locally the hand command still works and is still the fastest way:

            bin/build.sh -pl :parallel-consumer-proxy -am -DskipTests

        The Rust test fails with that command in its message rather than skipping, because a test
        that quietly does not run is not a passing test.

        `protoc` is required too: the crate generates from the frozen .proto in build.rs. Its
        header names the three places it looks, one of which is the copy Maven itself downloads.

        CARGO AND MAVEN SHARE target/, SO `mvn clean` DELETES THE RUST BUILD OUTPUT and the next
        foreign build recompiles the dependency tree from scratch (about a minute; the downloaded
        crates themselves are cached in ~/.cargo and are not re-fetched). Left deliberately: a
        clean that leaves a foreign toolchain's output behind is a clean that lies, and moving
        cargo's output elsewhere would need a .cargo/config.toml whose only purpose is to hide from
        the clean.
    -->
    <properties>
        <pc.foreign.skip>false</pc.foreign.skip>
        <pc.foreign.build.executable>cargo</pc.foreign.build.executable>
        <pc.foreign.build.args>clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings</pc.foreign.build.args>
        <pc.foreign.test.executable>cargo</pc.foreign.test.executable>
        <pc.foreign.test.args>test</pc.foreign.test.args>
    </properties>

    <profiles>
        <!--
            THE SIDECAR THE END-TO-END TEST DRIVES, AND WHY IT IS A DEPENDENCY.

            tests/harness spawns the real test-mode sidecar over the real wire, which needs the
            proxy module's test jar (TestModeMain and the harness) and its main jar. Declaring them
            here is what makes the CI row's own command build the proxy before this module's test
            phase; the row runs `package` rather than `test` for the other half of it, because the
            harness looks for jar FILES and `test` stops one phase short of producing them.

            SCOPED TO THIS PROFILE DELIBERATELY. No client depends on the engine, and with the
            profile inactive - every ordinary build - there is no dependency edge here at all, so a
            scoped build still runs no Rust toolchain and never reaches the engine. Modelled on the
            go-e2e-harness profile, which was written when the Go row failed this same way.

            The crate obtains the protocol the way every non-JVM client does, by generating from
            the .proto by path in build.rs, with no Maven dependency.
        -->
        <profile>
            <id>rust-e2e-harness</id>
            <activation>
                <property>
                    <name>pc.foreignClients</name>
                </property>
            </activation>
            <dependencies>
                <dependency>
                    <groupId>bz.stub.parallelconsumer</groupId>
                    <artifactId>parallel-consumer-proxy</artifactId>
                    <version>${project.version}</version>
                    <scope>test</scope>
                </dependency>
                <dependency>
                    <groupId>bz.stub.parallelconsumer</groupId>
                    <artifactId>parallel-consumer-proxy</artifactId>
                    <version>${project.version}</version>
                    <type>test-jar</type>
                    <scope>test</scope>
                </dependency>
                <dependency>
                    <!-- LongPollingMockConsumer and the harness's logback-test.xml. Test scope does
                         not travel transitively, so the proxy's own test dependencies are not ours
                         unless named - and the sidecar starts, then dies without them. -->
                    <groupId>bz.stub.parallelconsumer</groupId>
                    <artifactId>parallel-consumer-core</artifactId>
                    <version>${project.version}</version>
                    <type>test-jar</type>
                    <scope>test</scope>
                </dependency>
            </dependencies>
            <build>
                <plugins>
                    <plugin>
                        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                        <artifactId>maven-dependency-plugin</artifactId>
                        <!-- version from the root pom's pluginManagement -->
                        <executions>
                            <execution>
                                <!--
                                    THE HARNESS ASKS MAVEN FOR THIS LIST ITSELF IF IT IS NOT HERE,
                                    AND IN CI THAT SECOND ASK FAILS. Its fallback runs a nested
                                    `./mvnw -pl :parallel-consumer-proxy dependency:build-classpath`
                                    with no -am, so parallel-consumer-core has to come from the
                                    local repository - which a CI job never installs into, since
                                    the reactor stops at `package`. Writing the file here, from the
                                    reactor that already has every module resolved, means the
                                    fallback is only ever the developer's path. Same arrangement as
                                    the Go module's sidecar-classpath.txt, one filename apart
                                    because each harness chose its own.
                                -->
                                <id>proxy-test-classpath</id>
                                <!-- After the dependencies above resolve, before the test phase
                                     where the foreign suite runs and reads the file. -->
                                <phase>generate-test-resources</phase>
                                <goals>
                                    <goal>build-classpath</goal>
                                </goals>
                                <configuration>
                                    <outputFile>${project.build.directory}/proxy-test-classpath.txt</outputFile>
                                    <includeScope>test</includeScope>
                                </configuration>
                            </execution>
                        </executions>
                    </plugin>
                </plugins>
            </build>
        </profile>
    </profiles>

    <build>
        <plugins>
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.codehaus.mojo</groupId>
                <artifactId>exec-maven-plugin</artifactId>
            </plugin>
            <!--
                NO maven-clean-plugin FILESET HERE, AND THAT IS A MEASURED RESULT RATHER THAN AN
                OMISSION - the note exists because "nothing to clean" and "nobody wired the clean"
                look identical in a pom (astubbs#242).

                CARGO ALREADY WRITES WHERE MAVEN ALREADY CLEANS. This crate declares no workspace and
                no build.target-dir, so cargo's output directory is target/ beside Cargo.toml - the
                same path as ${project.build.directory}, which the default clean removes whole. The
                header above records the same collision from the other side, as the reason a `mvn
                clean` costs the next foreign build about a minute of recompilation. Proven both
                ways rather than reasoned about: target/debug/conformance-runner - the artefact the
                shared conformance suite prebuilds by asking cargo to build that one binary target
                (grep `conformance-runner` in the conformance module's LanguageRunners for the exact
                argument list, which an XML comment may not repeat: it contains two consecutive
                dashes) - is present after a build and gone after `mvn clean`.

                THE FETCHED CRATES ARE NOT OUTPUT and stay: the registry and its sources live in
                ~/.cargo, which is this language's ~/.m2, and `mvn clean` does not empty ~/.m2.
                `cargo clean` is not used either - it needs the toolchain present to delete files,
                and the whole premise of the foreign-clients profile is that the toolchain is absent
                on an ordinary machine. Cargo.lock is committed source, not output.

                So if a future change here moves cargo's output - a .cargo/config.toml setting
                target-dir, a workspace root above this crate - the clean silently stops working,
                and a fileset must be added below.
            -->
        </plugins>
    </build>

</project>
